title: Benchmark Infrastructure

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/BenchAlgI.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/CoreBench.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/Crc.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/Utils.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-02-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Benchmark Infrastructure}

      This tour revolves around the {[cu]CoreBench} module &ndash; used earlier within the {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} program.

      We'll first examine {[cu]BenchAlgI} &ndash; an **EMS** interface imple&shy;ment&shy;ed by {[cu]CoreBench} and the other algorithm modules.

      We'll then visit the supporting {[cu]Crc} and {[cu]Utils} modules before circling back to {[cu]CoreBench}.

      {[in]The modules track content found in the %[cx]core_util.c% and %[cx]coremark.h% legacy source files.}<br>

      {[in]%[cu]Crc% and %[cu]Utils% largely mirror design and implementa&shy;tion choices enshrined forever in <b>CoreMark</b>.}

  - cmds:
      - close 5
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |
      {[ht,$look]&thinsp;%[cu]BenchAlgI% &ndash; Quick Look}

      The {[cu]BenchAlgI} interface defines a common set of func&shy;tions implemented by all benchmark algorithm modules:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]CoreBench}&nbsp; [examined in this tour]<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]ListBench},{[cu]MatrixBench},{[cu]StateBench}&nbsp; [in next tour]

      The {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} program viewed earlier illustra&shy;ted calls to {[cf]setup} and {[cf]run} through the {[cu]CoreBench} module.

      {[in]%[cf]kind% and %[cf]print% primarily support program debug.}

      In general, {[cu]CoreBench} will forward client calls to its {[cu]BenchAlgI} functions to the other algorithm modules.

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 6
      - mark 4 B 13
      - mark 4 C 21
      - mark 4 D 28
      - mark 4 E 32
      - mark 4 F 36
      - mark 4 G 41
    focus: [4, 9]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Utils% &ndash; Details}

      The {[cu]Utils} module exports a common set of constants, types, and accessors used by its sibling **EMS** units.

      {[bm,A]} Each module implementing {[cu]BenchAlgI}.{[cf]kind} returns a distinct value of this type (with {[cn]ZZZ_} as a sentinel).

      {[bm,B]} Two {[cs]$table} datasets (initialized at build-time) respec&shy;tively hold {[ct]seed_t} con&shy;stants and {[ct]sum_t} variables.

      Both datasets in turn support special accessor functions:

      {[cn]seed_tab} &ndash; writeable only at build-time {[bm,C]}, readable at run-time {[bm,F]} through a forced {[ct]volatile_t} memory fetch.

      {[cn]crc_tab} &ndash; readable {[bm,E]} and writeable {[bm,G]} at run-time, special {[cf]bindCrc} function {[bm,D]} enforces single-assignment.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 10
      - mark 3 B 17
      - mark 3 C 23
    focus: [3, 12]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Crc% &ndash; Details}

      Updating 16-bit checksums serves as the primary means to ensure correct execution of the **CoreMark** algorithms.

      Each {[cu]BenchAlgI}.{[cf]run} implementation returns a {[ct]sum_t}, further combined with other checksums in the final result.

      The {[cu]Crc} module exports functions for respec&shy;tively accu&shy;mulating 16-bit {[bm,A]} and 32-bit {[bm,B]} in a given checksum.

      Both of these functions ultimately call {[cf]update}{[bm,C]}, which accepts a {[ct]u8} value applied bitwise to modify the {[cn]crc}.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 35
      - mark 2 B 39
      - mark 2 C 46
      - mark 2 D 51
      - mark 2 E 56
      - mark 2 F 60
    focus: [2, 37]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]CoreBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$targ% Details}

      The {[cu]BenchAlgI}.{[cf]run} implementation shown here lines up with the {[cf]iterate} function within `core_main.c`.

      Calls to {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} at {[bm,C]} and {[bm,D]} yield {[ct]sum_t} values accumulated in the {[cn]FINAL} checksum.

      Following **CoreMark**, line {[bm,E]} binds the current {[cn]FINAL} sum to {[cn]LIST} on the *first* call to {[cu]CoreBench}.{[cf]run}.

      The {[cf]print} {[bm,B]} and {[cf]setup} {[bm,F]} implementations call cor&shy;responding {[cu]BenchAlgI} functions in the other algorithms.

      The {[cf]kind} function at {[bm,A]} (used heavily within the body of {[cf]run}) returns a prescribed value of type {[ct]UT}.{[ct]Kind}.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 9
      - mark 2 B 17
      - mark 2 C 22
      - mark 2 D 26
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]CoreBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$meta% Details}

      Without violating the intent of legacy **CoreMark** sources, {[cu]CoreBench} executes some *build-time* computation.

      The {[cs]em$configure} meta-function at {[bm,B]} configures the amount of memory allotted to each benchmark algorithm.

      This function also binds {[cu]ListBench} at {[bm,C]} to its sibling {[cu]BenchAlgI} implementations via its {[cn]Bench&lbrack;01&rbrack;} proxies.

      The {[cs]em$construct} meta-function at {[bm,D]} initializes the first three {[ct]seed_t} values to prescribed **CoreMark** constants.

      {[in]If you haven't already noticed, we've defined a local %[ct]UT% moniker for %[cu]Utils% at line %[bm,A]% to improve readability.}

  - cmds:
      - close 5
      - close 4
      - close 3
      - close 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Look ahead at how {[cu]MatrixBench} satisfies {[cu]BenchAlgI}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Use {[cd]printf} to trace the execution of {[cu]Crc}.{[cf]update}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Determine the time {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} spends in {[cu]Crc}.

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      {[cu]BenchAlgI},&thinsp;{[cu]Crc}, and&thinsp;{[cu]Utils} play a similar role in sha&shy;ping the implementation of the three **CoreMark** algorithms.

      The next tour of the ****CoreMark**** group will explore the {[cu]MatrixBench} and {[cu]StateBench} algorithms.

      {[in]We'll then devote a separate tour to %[cu]ListBench%, due to the relative complexity of this algorithm.}

      {[hc]}
